#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd9cf94f-6d2c-5279-b476-55c7434eefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd9cf94f-6d2c-5279-b476-55c7434eefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37a43fd-f6c9-58a0-8e79-34180df6e0a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f0a9b7d-a8ca-55a3-9a38-83e9ea72a8f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332730" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ff7f935-dcec-5b4c-ba1d-e0b4c9e197a4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Functions of X as Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c1ce28-8bfe-5b09-a15d-7c4b76ecfa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490} {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} {:answer/id #uuid "a553f444-e68c-53b4-8d93-575a4cc7cf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbe008-7f1a-5a31-9cb7-f9dd2c67fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310318} {:answer/id #uuid "ec46c1a1-e0b3-5094-a3be-1a97aa9a9b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1bdc7f-51da-5393-bc6a-4b97c1c1ced8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a1235f2-6c7a-5f14-b04f-a38d354a3d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337837" :question/problem "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
